### Site specifics 

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;">

There are minor differences between the sites.

- CPU/GPU types
- installed modules
- interactivity

Examples: Tetralith (NSC), Dardel (KTH), Kebnekaise (HPC2N)

-----

#### Site specifics - Tetralith 

<small style="width: 85%; position: relative; bottom: 100px;">

- 32 cores/node
- 96-384 GB RAM/node
- Nvidia T4 GPUs (1/node)
    -
```bash
#SBATCH -n 1
#SBATCH -c 32
#SBATCH --gpus-per-task=1
```

-----

#### Site specifics - Dardel 

<small style="width: 85%; position: relative; bottom: 100px;">

- 128 cores/node
- 256-2048 GB RAM/node
- 4 AMD Instinct™ MI250X á 2 GCDs per node
    -
```bash
#SBATCH -N 1
#SBATCH --ntasks-per-node=1
#SBATCH -p gpu
```

-----

#### Site specifics - Kebnekaise 

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 90px;">

- 28 cores/skylake, 72 cores/largemem, 128/256 cores/AMD Zen3/Zen4
- 128-3072 GB RAM/node
- NVidia V100, A100, A6000, L40s, H100, A40, AMD MI100
    -
```bash
#SBATCH --gpus=x
#SBATCH -C <type>
```
    - type and number of cards/node (x)
        - v100, 2
        - a40, 8
        - a6000, 2
        - l40s, 2 or 6
        - a100, 2
        - h100, 4
        - mi100, 2

-----

### Batch jobs

<small style="width: 85%; position: relative; bottom: 100px;">

Serial job:

```bash
#!/bin/bash 
#SBATCH -A <account>
#SBATCH -t HHH:MM:SS 
#SBATCH -n 1

module load <modules>

./myserialprogram
```

- n, number of tasks. Since cores per task is 1 as default, this equals cores in this case.

-----

#### Note: Time/walltime

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;">

- the job **will** terminate when the time runs out, whether it has finished or not
- you will only be "charged" for the consumed time
- asking for more time than needed will make the job take longer to start
- short jobs can start quicker (backfill)
- if you have no idea how long your job takes, ask for "long" time
- Conclusion: Ask for "a bit" more time than needed, but not too much

-----

### Batch jobs

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;">

MPI job:

```bash
#!/bin/bash 
#SBATCH -A <account>
#SBATCH -t HHH:MM:SS 
#SBATCH -n <tasks> 

module load <modules>

srun ./mympiprogram
```

- Other options are asking for whole nodes (- N) and possibly asking for ``--tasks-per-node``
- ``srun`` and ``mpirun`` should be interchangeable at HPC2N. Tetralith uses ``mpprun`` and Dardel uses ``srun``
- Remember you need to load a module with MPI

-----

### Batch jobs

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;">

OpenMP job:

```bash
#!/bin/bash 
#SBATCH -A <account>
#SBATCH -t HHH:MM:SS 
#SBATCH -c <cores-per-task> 

module load <modules>

# Set OMP_NUM_THREADS to the same value as -c with a fallback in case it isn't set.
# SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK is set to the value of -c, but only if -c is explicitly set
if [ -n "$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK" ]; then
  omp_threads=$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK
else
  omp_threads=1
fi
export OMP_NUM_THREADS=$omp_threads

./myopenmpprogram
```

- ``-c`` is used to set cores per task and should be the same as ``OMP_NUM_THREADS``

-----

### Using GPUs

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;">

This is the most different of the Slurm settings, between centers.

| Resource | batch settings |
| -------- | -------------- |
| Tetralith | ``#SBATCH -n 1`` <br> ``#SBATCH -c 32`` <br> ``#SBATCH --gpus-per-task=1`` |
| Dardel    | ``#SBATCH -N 1`` <br> ``#SBATCH --ntasks-per-node=1`` <br> ``#SBATCH -p gpu`` |
| Kebnekaise | ``#SBATCH --gpus=x`` <br> ``#SBATCH -C <type>`` |

-----

### Dependencies

<small style="width: 85%; position: relative; bottom: 100px;">

Sometimes your workflow has jobs that depend on a previous job. This can be handled through Slurm:

- Submit your first job: ``sbatch my-job.sh``
- Wait for that job to finish before starting next job: <br>``sbatch -d afterok:<prev-JOBID> my-next-job.sh``

If you have several/many jobs connected like this it is probably a good idea to script it.

-----

### Increasing the memory per task 

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;">

- Running out of memory ("OOM"):
    - usually the job stops ("crashes")
    - check the Slurm error/log files
    - check with sacct/seff/jobstats/job-usage depending on cluster
- Fixes:
    - use "fat" nodes
    - allocate more cores just for memory
    - tweak mem usage in app, if possible

-----

#### Increasing memory per task - Tetralith 

96 GB/node:
```bash
-C thin --exclusive
```

384 GB/node:
```bash
-C fat --exclusive
```

More cores/task, some just giving memory (MPI):
```bash
sbatch --ntasks-per-node=16 --cpus-per-task=2
```

-----

#### Increasing memory per task - Dardel 

<small style="width: 95%; position: relative; bottom: 80px;">

| Type | RAM | Partition | Available | Example flag |
| ---- | --- | --------- | --------- | ------------ |
| Thin | 256 GB | main, shared, long | 227328 MB | |
| Large | 512 GB | main, memory | 456704 MB | --mem=440GB |
| Huge | 1 TB | main, memory | 915456 MB | --mem=880GB |
| Giant | 2 TB | memory | 1832960 MB | --mem=1760GB |
| GPU | 512 GB | gpu | 456704 MB | --mem=440GB |

On shared partitions you need to give number of cores and will get RAM equivalent for that

-----

